# backend/.env.production.example
# Copy to backend/.env.production and fill in. The real file is gitignored.
# Generate FRESH secrets — never reuse the dev values that were committed/pasted.
#
# This file is loaded by the `migrate`, `backend`, `celery_worker` and
# `celery_beat` services in BOTH docker-compose.prod.yml and
# docker-compose.aws.yml (`env_file:`). It is the only env file the backend
# reads in production.
#
# It does NOT configure the frontend. Every VITE_* variable is inlined by Vite
# at BUILD time and must be exported in the shell before `docker compose up
# --build` — see the "Shell exports" block at the bottom of this file. This is
# the most common way a production deploy looks healthy and is missing a
# feature: the containers are up, the page renders, and the copilot is absent
# because VITE_COPILOT_ENABLED defaulted to false at build time.

# --- Core DB --- (the `db` service on the compose network; or a managed URL)
# docker-compose.prod.yml: the password here MUST match the POSTGRES_PASSWORD
# you export for compose. docker-compose.aws.yml: there is no db container, so
# the RDS password lives only inside this URL and ?sslmode=require is required.
# The Postgres MUST have the pgvector extension available (corpus retrieval).
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://uvs_app:CHANGE_ME_DB_PASSWORD@your-db.rds.amazonaws.com:5432/uni_volunteer?sslmode=require

# Connection pool. (pool_size + max_overflow) × uvicorn workers, plus Celery,
# must stay under the database's max_connections — the prod compose files run
# `--workers 4`, so these values mean up to 40 backend connections plus the
# worker's own. Check the ceiling before raising them; on a small RDS instance
# max_connections is derived from instance memory and is lower than you expect.
# db_statement_timeout_ms is the per-query cap the API runs under; Celery sets
# its own, longer one.
DB_POOL_SIZE=5
DB_MAX_OVERFLOW=5
DB_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS=15000

# --- JWT --- generate with: python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(32))"
JWT_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_FRESH_64_CHAR_HEX
JWT_ALGORITHM=HS256
ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRES_MINUTES=60
REFRESH_TOKEN_EXPIRES_DAYS=14

# --- Redis / Celery --- (the `redis` service; or a managed URL)
# Three separate databases on one Redis: cache, broker, results.
# celery_worker and celery_beat are load-bearing — reminders, broadcast pacing,
# the idle-session sweep and copilot profile extraction all run there.
REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379/0
CELERY_BROKER_URL=redis://redis:6379/1
CELERY_RESULT_BACKEND=redis://redis:6379/2

# --- Email --- two supported transports, chosen by EMAIL_MODE:
#   smtp     -> smtplib (AWS SES SMTP in production, Mailpit in dev)
#   sendgrid -> the SendGrid HTTPS API
# Set the block for the mode you chose and leave the other blank. The unused
# keys are harmless; a blank key in the mode you ARE using is not — outbound
# mail then fails per-message inside Celery, where nothing user-facing shows it.
EMAIL_MODE=smtp

# SES SMTP (EMAIL_MODE=smtp). Port 587 + STARTTLS.
# SMTP_USERNAME / SMTP_PASSWORD are SES *SMTP credentials* — generated in the
# SES console under "SMTP settings > Create SMTP credentials". They are NOT the
# IAM access key ID / secret; pasting the IAM key here fails auth on every send.
SMTP_HOST=email-smtp.us-west-2.amazonaws.com
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USE_TLS=true
SMTP_USERNAME=CHANGE_ME_SES_SMTP_USERNAME
SMTP_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME_SES_SMTP_PASSWORD
# Must be an address (or a domain) VERIFIED in SES, in the same region as
# SMTP_HOST. While the SES account is in the sandbox, every RECIPIENT must be
# verified too — so a sandboxed account delivers to the team and silently
# rejects volunteers.
EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=scitrek@yourdomain.org

# SendGrid (EMAIL_MODE=sendgrid). Leave blank when EMAIL_MODE=smtp.
SENDGRID_API_KEY=

# --- Public URLs --- (emails, magic links, QR check-in, CORS). Use real https URLs.
# FRONTEND_URL is the ONLY key config.py reads for the frontend origin
# (frontend_base_url is a read-only alias for it, not a separate setting).
# FRONTEND_BASE_URL is NOT read (config.py's extra="ignore" silently drops
# it) — setting only that key leaves every magic link, reset link, invite
# link, and ICS URL pointing at the http://localhost:5173 default.
FRONTEND_URL=https://scitrek.example.org
BACKEND_BASE_URL=https://scitrek.example.org
# Comma-separated. The SPA is served from the same origin as the API behind
# Caddy, so this is normally just the one domain.
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://scitrek.example.org

# --- Rate limiting --- per IP per path, Redis-backed. Fails OPEN when Redis is
# unreachable, deliberately: an outage must not stop signups.
RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=60
RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS=100

# --- Login lockout --- per account, so it still bites while the per-IP limiter
# above is failing open. 10 wrong passwords locks the account for 15 minutes.
# A locked account gets the same 401 as a wrong password, on purpose — a distinct
# reply would tell an attacker which staff addresses are real. That makes the
# audit log the only place the lock is visible: look for `user_login_locked` if
# somebody reports being unable to log in with the right password.
LOGIN_MAX_FAILED_ATTEMPTS=10
LOGIN_LOCKOUT_MINUTES=15

# --- Venue check-in codes (W5 S-02) --- ceiling on wrong guesses, per
# (event, caller address). The code is only four digits and it is the sole gate
# on four no-auth check-in endpoints. Keyed per caller, NOT per event, so nobody
# can burn the ceiling to shut down check-in for a whole classroom.
VENUE_CODE_MAX_ATTEMPTS=10
VENUE_CODE_ATTEMPT_WINDOW_SECONDS=900

# --- Magic links --- the account-less signup path. TTL is short on purpose:
# the link is a bearer credential sitting in an inbox. The two ceilings below
# are what stop the app being used as a mailbomb relay against a third party.
MAGIC_LINK_TTL_MINUTES=15
MAGIC_LINK_MAX_PER_EMAIL_PER_HOUR=5
MAGIC_LINK_MAX_PER_IP_PER_HOUR=20

# --- Copilot (v1.4) ---
# The backend half of the feature flag. The FRONTEND half is
# VITE_COPILOT_ENABLED, a build arg — see the bottom of this file. BOTH are
# required: this key alone leaves the endpoints live and the drawer invisible.
COPILOT_ENABLED=true
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=CHANGE_ME_OPENROUTER_KEY
# Free tiers only — a paid slug on a free key 403s ("Key limit exceeded") on
# every message. OpenRouter retires ":free" tiers without notice, and when it
# does BOTH primary and fallback 404 and the drawer only ever prints
# "Stream failed: NotFoundError". Re-check the live list rather than guessing a
# slug — the curl one-liner is in app/config.py, and its `tools` filter is not
# optional (the agent loop needs function calling).
COPILOT_PRIMARY_MODEL=nvidia/nemotron-3-ultra-550b-a55b:free
COPILOT_FALLBACK_MODEL=google/gemma-4-31b-it:free
COPILOT_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60
# 1024 was fine when the copilot only answered questions. An agent turn spends
# the same budget writing tool arguments, and a real request — fifteen bookable
# shifts, each with day, times and capacity — ran out mid-word while still
# returning a syntactically whole tool call.
COPILOT_MAX_COMPLETION_TOKENS=8192
# LLM-surface guardrails. Per-user messages/minute on chat; a separate ceiling
# on confirms, which is the endpoint that actually EXECUTES the agent's writes;
# and an org-wide tokens/day budget (0 disables it).
COPILOT_RATE_LIMIT_MESSAGES_PER_MINUTE=10
COPILOT_RATE_LIMIT_CONFIRMS_PER_MINUTE=20
COPILOT_RATE_LIMIT_FEEDBACK_PER_MINUTE=30
COPILOT_DAILY_TOKEN_BUDGET=500000
# The copilot's two mail tools (send_reminder_email, nudge_understaffed_module)
# are now wired to the same Celery + SMTP path as every other email, so this
# flag is live: ON means the agent can put real mail in the queue.
#
# It ships OFF anyway. Turn it on only once SES is out of the sandbox and the
# sending domain is verified, because these two tools are the only place where
# the *audience* is chosen by a model reading a sentence rather than by a
# human. MAX_OUTBOUND_RECIPIENTS is the blast radius if it reads one wrong;
# volunteers who turned reminder email off are skipped regardless.
#
# The tools report queued_count, not sent_count — a queued message is not a
# delivered one, and the copilot is instructed to say so.
COPILOT_OUTBOUND_EMAIL_ENABLED=false
COPILOT_MAX_OUTBOUND_RECIPIENTS=200
# End-of-session profile extraction (cross-session "remembers you" memory).
# ON as of 2026-08-20, when the OpenRouter account was funded.
#
# This shipped off for one reason: an unfunded key allows ~50 free-model
# requests/day for everything, and an unattended LLM call per closed session
# could spend the day's allowance, leaving a real question answered with a
# rate-limit error the user could not account for. Funding raised the ceiling
# to ~1000/day; at SciTrek's volume extraction is a few percent of that, so
# that objection is spent.
#
# The other half of K31 was fixed in code rather than argued away: the task
# used autoretry_for=(Exception,), which retried rate-limit and quota errors
# — the job that ran out of requests responded by making more, and on top of
# app/copilot/llm.py's own three primary->fallback sweeps that came to as many
# as 24 provider calls for one profile. Provider errors now end the task on
# the first attempt; only non-provider failures retry.
#
# Note config.py still defaults this to false. That is deliberate: forgetting
# the variable should cost a nicety, not start unattended LLM calls on
# somebody's key. Production opts in here, explicitly.
COPILOT_PROFILE_EXTRACTION_ENABLED=true
# The tool-using agent loop (Phase B). ON — this is now the default in
# config.py, and the key is listed here so the choice stays visible.
#
# Decided 2026-08-06 (see the DECIDED box in .planning/FINAL-ROADMAP.md, W3):
# the copilot ships as a full tool-using agent, not retrieval-grounded Q&A.
# 34 tools, a confirmation-card flow, and a per-turn token ledger. Off is
# not an option for SciTrek: a copilot that cannot act is not the feature.
#
# What to watch, because it is on rather than despite it: the tool layer's
# tests all drive it through a monkeypatch, so real-model behaviour is
# established by manual exercise, not by the suite. Exercise a full write
# turn — propose, confirm, apply — before you trust it in front of staff.
# The OpenRouter account was funded on 2026-08-20, so the ceiling is roughly
# 1000 requests/day rather than 50. An agent turn spends several, which is
# comfortable at SciTrek's volume — but it is a ceiling, not headroom, and
# app/copilot/llm.py sweeps primary->fallback up to three times per call, so
# a turn that keeps failing costs more than it looks like.
#
# Setting this to false is a safe rollback at any time: K23 makes the
# disabled path return a real 503 with a message rather than the bare 500
# it used to, and nothing persists that a restart cannot ignore. If you do
# roll back, the drawer header ("live rosters", "waits for your approval")
# becomes false and needs reverting with it.
#
# The two mail tools remain refused regardless — that is
# COPILOT_OUTBOUND_EMAIL_ENABLED above, a separate decision.
COPILOT_AGENT_LOOP_ENABLED=true
# Warm both local models on a background thread at startup so the first real
# question after a deploy doesn't wait for ~1.3GB to load. The weights are baked
# into the image (BAKE_MODEL_WEIGHTS=1, the Dockerfile default), so this is a
# disk read, not a download. Failures only log — readiness never waits on it.
#
# OFF BY DEFAULT, AND ONLY TURN IT ON ONCE THE BOX IS SIZED FOR IT. Resident
# models cost ~880MB *per uvicorn worker* (the app idles at ~310MB), and the
# prod compose files run --workers 4, so budget ~900MB per worker: ~4GB before
# anything else on the host. Below that this is an unconditional OOM, and a
# nasty one: the load runs on a background thread, so startup completes, the
# healthcheck passes, the deploy reports success, and the instance dies seconds
# later. Leaving it false costs only a slow first question — the models then
# load lazily on demand. See docs/deployment.md → "Memory budget".
COPILOT_PREWARM_ON_STARTUP=false

# --- Corpus / retrieval ---
# Must match how the shipped corpus was embedded. Both retrieval CTEs filter
# chunks by provider, so a wrong value here reads zero rows from BOTH halves of
# hybrid retrieval: no citations, no error, no clue. Every shipped chunk is
# local-bge, so 'local' is the only value that works out of the box.
CORPUS_EMBEDDING_PRIMARY=local
CORPUS_EMBEDDING_FALLBACK=local
# Locked at 1024 — the vector(1024) column on corpus_chunks cannot change
# without a full re-embed.
CORPUS_EMBEDDING_DIMENSIONS=1024
# Only needed if CORPUS_EMBEDDING_PRIMARY=jina. Leave blank otherwise.
JINA_API_KEY=
# Origin used to build a citation's click-through URL from a chunk's
# source_path. Blank = citations return no path, which is the safe default: it
# avoids publishing internal repo paths. Opt in with e.g.
# https://github.com/Anteater10/uni-volunteer-scheduler/blob/main
CORPUS_SOURCE_ORIGIN_URL=

# --- Seed admin --- (created by the `migrate` step). Use a strong password.
# This is the account you first log in as. SEED_ADMIN_NAME is optional.
SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@yourdomain.org
SEED_ADMIN_NAME=SciTrek Admin
SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME_STRONG_ADMIN_PASSWORD

# --- Ops ---
LOG_LEVEL=INFO
# Error monitoring (optional). Empty = off. Create a free Sentry project and
# paste its DSN to capture unhandled API + Celery exceptions.
SENTRY_DSN=

# --- PRODUCTION SAFETY ---
DEBUG=false
# ENVIRONMENT is a Literal["development","staging","production"] — a typo like
# `prod` is a startup failure, not a silent downgrade. It disables /docs and
# makes the app REFUSE TO BOOT if EXPOSE_TOKENS_FOR_TESTING is set. Both prod
# compose files also set it on the service; keeping it here covers non-compose
# deployments (a PaaS has no compose file, and that is exactly how a live
# instance was once found running with /docs public).
ENVIRONMENT=production
# DO NOT set EXPOSE_TOKENS_FOR_TESTING in production. Its absence = off.
# (When ON, signup responses leak auth tokens — dev/E2E only.)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT IN THIS FILE: shell exports needed BEFORE `docker compose up --build`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The three VITE_* / DOMAIN values below are build-time and compose-time, not
# backend runtime, so they cannot live here. Export them in the shell on the
# host in the same session you run compose from:
#
#   export DOMAIN='scitrek.example.org'          # A record -> Elastic IP
#   export VITE_API_URL="https://${DOMAIN}"
#   export VITE_COPILOT_ENABLED=true             # <-- defaults to FALSE
#
#   # docker-compose.prod.yml only (local db container; not the AWS/RDS file):
#   export POSTGRES_PASSWORD='<same password as in DATABASE_URL>'
#
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.aws.yml up -d --build
#
# VITE_COPILOT_ENABLED is the one with no guard. DOMAIN and VITE_API_URL use
# compose's `:?` syntax and abort the build if unset; VITE_COPILOT_ENABLED
# falls back to `false`, so forgetting it produces a completely healthy deploy
# with no copilot FAB, no admin Copilot nav item, and no feedback page — while
# COPILOT_ENABLED=true above makes the backend look correctly configured.
#
# Vite inlines these at BUILD time. Changing one means REBUILDING the frontend
# image: `docker compose -f <file> up -d --build frontend`. A plain `restart`
# will not pick up a new value.
